+# Feature Specification: JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+**Status**: Implemented
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+
+### User Story 1 - Apply a patch to a document (P1)
+A service developer calls `applyPatch(document, patch)` and gets the patched document as a new value.
+
+**Acceptance scenarios**:
+1. The RFC 6902 Appendix A examples (A.1–A.16) produce the documented results or errors.
+2. The input document and patch are never mutated.
+
+### User Story 2 - Invalid patches and failed operations throw (P1)
+1. A patch that is not an array, an operation that is not an object, an unknown/missing `op`, a missing or non-string `path`/`from`, or a missing `value` (add/replace/test) throws.
+2. Operations that fail (remove/replace of a missing target, failed `test`, move/copy from a missing location, add into a missing parent, bad array index) throw; no partial result is returned.
+
+## Requirements
+- **FR-001**: `applyPatch` is exported from `src/index.ts`; operations apply sequentially.
+- **FR-002**: `add`: adds/replaces an object member; inserts into an array at index ≤ length, or appends with `-`; path `""` replaces the whole document.
+- **FR-003**: `remove` and `replace` require the target to exist (array `-` never exists).
+- **FR-004**: `move` = remove from `from` then add at `path`; `from` must not be a proper prefix of `path`; same `from` and `path` is a no-op if it exists.
+- **FR-005**: `copy` adds a deep copy of the value at `from`.
+- **FR-006**: `test` compares by JSON equality (object member order ignored, arrays ordered, types strict) and throws on inequality.
+- **FR-007**: Pointers use the RFC 6901 parsing already in `src/index.ts`; members named `__proto__` are ordinary data.
+- **FR-008**: Input is not mutated; results do not share structure that callers could mutate through the input.
